  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\n- Periods of Rain and Thunderstorms Into Next Week\n\nThe moisture and instability axis with the front currently extends\nfrom north central IL into the eastern UP. This is where \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms have developed over the \npast few hours. This axis will slowly shift east into western \nMichigan this morning. MUCAPE values around 500 to 1000 J/kg will \nadvect into the area throughout the day as well continuing the \npotential for thunder. Not expecting any severe storms with shear \naround 20 to 30 knots. However precipitable water values \napproaching 2 inches and the slow moving front could result in \ntraining over the same area creating an environment where some \nspots could see higher rainfall amounts in the 1-3 inch range. \nOverall trends for these higher amounts have decreased slightly, \nbut the HREF still shows a signal.\n\nA lull in showers and thunderstorms is possible this afternoon\nbefore we see another surge of positive vorticity advection move\nin this evening. Instability drops off around 11pm with scattered\nshowers likely continuing across much of the area overnight.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will likely be focused along and south\nof a Holland to Alma line during the day Thursday. Instability on\nthe HREF picks up a little higher than today with values around\n1000 to 1500 J/kg. Could see more of a diurnal trend with\nthunderstorm activity where chances become more likely during the\nafternoon and then drop off as we get closer to sunset.\n\nAfter Thursday the pattern becomes more uncertain as we remain in\na broad upper level trough with the potential for multiple \nshortwaves to move through the region. Each shortwave will bring \nchances (20 to 40 percent) for showers and thunderstorms. Not any \none day looks to be a washout as these shortwaves will quickly \nmove through. Either way, keep an eye on the forecast if you have \nany outdoor plans as timing will likely change as we get closer.",
